## Migration Analysis

Your code has these Thread.Abort patterns:

| Pattern | Location |
|---------|----------|
| **Cancellable work loop** | `while (_running)` + `Thread.Abort()` in `Stop()` |
| **ThreadAbortException as control flow** | Catch block calling `RollbackCurrentTransaction()` |
| **Thread.ResetAbort** | Preventing rethrow so `finally` runs cleanly |
| **Blocking call interruption** | `GetNextItem()` may block indefinitely |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            bool itemStarted = false;
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                itemStarted = true;
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Cancellation requested — roll back any partial work
                if (itemStarted)
                    RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit the loop cleanly
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 5 seconds for the thread to exit gracefully
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }

    // These methods must now accept and respect the token:
    private object GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
    {
        // Example: if using a BlockingCollection<T>:
        //   return _queue.Take(token);
        // If using a ManualResetEventSlim to signal availability:
        //   _itemAvailable.Wait(token);
        // If doing synchronous I/O that blocks:
        //   use token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(timeout) instead of Thread.Sleep
        throw new NotImplementedException();
    }

    private void ProcessItem(object item, CancellationToken token)
    {
        // Insert token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested() between
        // expensive steps to allow timely cancellation
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        // ... do work ...
    }
}
```

## Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **`GetNextItem()` blocks forever** | If it doesn't accept a `CancellationToken`, `Stop()` will hang. You *must* thread the token into blocking calls. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers/callbacks. Implement `IDisposable`. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` to avoid swallowing unrelated exceptions. |
| **Assuming cancellation is instant** | It only fires at the next checkpoint. Add `ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` between expensive steps in `ProcessItem`. |
| **Removing rollback logic** | The old `ThreadAbortException` catch did cleanup — make sure `RollbackCurrentTransaction()` is still called on cancellation. |

## What Changed and Why

- **`Thread.Abort()` → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`** — `Thread.Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on .NET 6+.
- **`ThreadAbortException` → `OperationCanceledException`** — the standard exception for cooperative cancellation.
- **`Thread.ResetAbort()` → removed** — no longer needed; `OperationCanceledException` doesn't auto-rethrow like `ThreadAbortException` did.
- **`volatile bool _running` → `CancellationToken`** — the token unblocks waiting calls; a plain bool cannot.
- **`Thread.Sleep(2000)` → `Thread.Join(timeout)`** — actually waits for the thread instead of hoping it finishes.
